The LLM side of the Vulkan workload lease, on
f280b26983ad0fdb705a0d9ebf0503e76f2899b0 after the six-patch production series
and llama-vulkan-view-alias-deps.patch.

The appliance admits one active qwen-owned Vulkan compute workload.
scripts/image-service.py holds ~/qwen-webui-state/vulkan-workload.lock with
flock(LOCK_EX) from job start to artifact rename, and at f280b269 it is the only
writer, so a chat turn and an image generation share the device. This patch
makes llama-server the second writer under the same lock.

The lease is two-sided in time rather than tied to residency. An idle loaded
server reserves memory while the device executes nothing, so residency alone
holds nothing; server_context_impl::update_slots owns the transitions, taking
the lease where its all_idle check finds a busy slot and releasing it where the
check finds none. Every graph a pass submits therefore runs inside the lease,
and the release trails the final decode by exactly one update_slots pass,
because a non-idle pass posts SERVER_TASK_TYPE_NEXT_RESPONSE and the pass that
observes the idle slots is the next one.

QWEN_VULKAN_WORKLOAD_LOCK names the file. An unset or empty variable leaves the
descriptor closed and every lease call inert, which is the state of every
llama-bench, probe, and standalone harness in this tree. A named path that
cannot be opened fails init(), because a server that skipped the lease silently
would run graphs beside a generation while its log said the invariant held. The
descriptor is opened once with O_CLOEXEC, so nothing the process spawns
inherits the open file description flock binds to, and init() reuses it when
handle_sleeping_state wakes the server after destroy().

A stall is logged while it lasts rather than after it. The acquire tries
LOCK_EX | LOCK_NB first; on EWOULDBLOCK it writes the waiting line naming the
path, then blocks, and the acquire line carries waited_ms measured across the
blocking call alone. server.cpp registers SIGINT and SIGTERM with sa_flags = 0,
so no SA_RESTART covers that block and a terminating signal returns EINTR: the
acquire logs the interruption and returns without the lease, which lets the task
queue observe the termination it was told about while a live queue retries on
its next pass. Looping on EINTR instead would leave qwen-teardown.sh reporting
residue for a server that can never unblock its main thread.

The process doing the work holds the lease. In router mode server.cpp leaves
ctx_server uninitialised -- load_model and therefore init() run only in the
non-router branch -- so the router parent opens nothing. server-models.cpp
snapshots environ into base_env at server_models_routes construction and spawns
each child with that copy plus LLAMA_SERVER_ROUTER_PORT, so the variable reaches
the child that owns the context and the graphs verbatim.
scripts/vulkan-runtime-env.sh scrubs the GGML_VK_*, display, vendor, and VK
layer names, so QWEN_VULKAN_WORKLOAD_LOCK crosses the exec boundary untouched.

The whole block compiles on POSIX alone, guarded the way server.cpp guards its
own sigaction block, since sys/file.h and flock have no Windows equivalent.

The hunk offsets encode the post-series line numbers, so the patch applies after
llama-vulkan-low-priority, llama-no-cpu-fallback, llama-vulkan-duty-cycle,
llama-vulkan-runtime-submit-limit, llama-vulkan-submit-trace,
llama-router-tools-proxy, and llama-vulkan-view-alias-deps, in that order.

